WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.290
There’s a scene in A Christmas Carol where
a couple charity workers visit Scrooge’s

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.790
office looking for donations.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.600
Of course, Scrooge gotta Scrooge, so he kicks
them to the curb.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.020
Ebenezer with all the money in the world, can’t spare
a dime.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.200
That should feel counterintuitive to us, but
as the audience, we nod our heads and think,

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.460
“yup, that makes sense.”

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.630
Dickens didn’t invent the trope of the cruel,
greedy rich guy, but he sure popularized it,

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:30.190
and there have been countless examples since:
Montgomery Burns, Mr. Potter, Gordon Gekko,

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:31.970
Smaug the Dragon, the Lannisters.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:37.170
The idea that one’s wealth is inversely
proportional to their empathy is a staple

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.859
of fiction, but is there a basis in reality
for it?

00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:43.489
Does having more money make you meaner?

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:45.960
And if so, what can be done about it?

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.660
If I asked you “why don’t you give away
more money to worthy causes?”, what would you say?

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.000
I’d guess we’d all have a response along
the lines of “I don’t have enough money.”

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.530
That sounds reasonable on its face.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.469
But what if I told you that research suggests
having more money makes you LESS generous?

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:15.510
A study by the Chronicle of Philanthropy found
households earning 50 to 70 thousand dollars

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:19.479
gave 7.6% of their discretionary spending
to charity.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:21.710
Those earning $100,000 gave 4.2%.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:27.270
And those who made over $200,000 and lived
in a zipcode where 40% of their neighbors

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.820
earned the same gave only 2.8% to charity.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.360
And a University of Notre Dame survey found
that Americans below the poverty line were

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.619
twice as likely to give up to 1% of their
entire income to charity compared to those

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:42.880
above the poverty line.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.420
Researchers at UC Berkeley conducted an experiment
where subjects were asked to rank themselves

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.670
on a socio-economic scale from low to high.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:56.009
Then they were given some cash and asked how
much of it they’d consider giving to a stranger.

00:01:56.009 --> 00:02:01.369
Again, people who identified themselves as
lower-class, gave away 44 percent more on

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:03.539
average than their higher ranked counterparts.

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:08.150
Perhaps you’ve heard about the famous “giving
pledge” campaign initiated by Warren Buffet,

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.100
Bill Gates, and other billionaires.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.540
In an inspiring move, they are trying to convince
other super wealthy families to give

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.570
away half of their fortune over their lifetime.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:21.300
But, sadly, out of the hundreds of thousands
of ultra-high net worth individuals in the

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:26.920
world, only 187 have signed the pledge in
the 8 years since the campaign started.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.500
There’s no denying there’s a “self-preservation”
streak when it comes to amassing wealth.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:37.240
Money doesn’t just make us more possessive,
it can also lead to obnoxious, arrogant social behavior.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.180
Take one study that watched
100 random partners play a rigged game of Monopoly.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.520
One of the two players started with double
the starting cash, collected twice as much

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.020
when they passed “Go”, and rolled more
dice.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.950
These advantaged players 
demonstrated more visible displays of victoriousness

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:59.330
like dominant physical gesturing and loudly
moving their pieces around the board.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:05.060
They even ate more pretzels that were specifically
located nearer to the disadvantaged player.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.310
When asked afterwards what factors led to
their success, many of them pointed to some

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.820
sort of inherent skill rather than the obvious
fact that the game was heavily tilted in their

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.190
favor from the start.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:21.140
It’s like their brains had to find some
sort of self-affirming justification for unearned privilege.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:26.800
Even pretend-wealth can turn nice Aunt Sharon
into a cutthroat real estate baron!

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.620
What’s going on here?

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.400
What is it about wealth that can turn even
the best of us into an un-caring Scrooge?

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:37.360
One of the major factors is the connection
between wealth and power.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.440
The greater your wealth, the more advantages
you have in life and the more power you have

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.010
over situations, circumstances, and people.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.970
That boost in our sense of power holds a key
to this phenomenon.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:53.950
In a study at McMaster University, subjects
were split into two groups - one primed to

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:59.080
feel powerful by recounting a situation where
they were in charge - and one was not.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:03.320
Those feeling more powerful were far less
likely to exhibit what neuroscientists call

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:08.140
“mirroring,” our subconscious copying
of others’ behaviors and moods.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.680
Laughing when others laugh.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.490
Feeling tension in the room when someone says
something inappropriate.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:15.020
Getting choked up watching someone else cry.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:19.190
Mirroring is a vital skill that allows us
to adapt appropriately to the people around

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.510
us, and fit into social situations.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.650
And it is an essential building block to allowing
us to feel empathy.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.210
If you’re wealthy, or hope to be one day
by watching Two Cents videos, this might be

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:31.210
a bit alarming.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.210
But fear not!

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:36.210
Now that you are aware of some of the dangers
that come along with wealth, there are steps you

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:41.090
can take to build empathy and remain generous
now matter how much money you have.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:46.030
For starters, don’t isolate yourself or
surround yourself with only your wealthy friends.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:50.440
Wealthy individuals usually live physically
further apart from others, and when they socialize,

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.060
it’s with people at their same income level.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.560
But studies show that wealthy people that
live in economically diverse neighborhoods

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.920
give away twice as much to charity than those
that don’t.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.150
So where you live makes a big difference!

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:05.990
And the same guys that did the monopoly study
also found that experiencing nature can make

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:07.280
us more generous.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.290
The feeling of awe when you look up at the
stars or over a vast, expansive landscape

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:16.840
makes you feel less important and therefore
more likely to help someone else out.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.970
So take a hike, Ebenezer!

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.290
Wealth isn’t evil.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:21.290
Or good.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:25.670
It’s a tool that can impact the world in
ways beneficial... and terrible.

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:30.420
As you journey towards a more prosperous future,
don’t forget to protect yourself from the

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.700
emotionally numbing effects of wealth.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.210
For example, you can set a specific percentage
of your income to give away - no matter how

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.260
much you make.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:43.400
And try to actively seek out situations that
will connect you people that aren’t like you.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:47.680
It’ll help make sure you own your money…
and it doesn’t own you.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.120
And that’s our two cents!

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:53.620
If you give to charity, how do you decide how much? And how do you stick to it?

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.320
Let us know in the comments.

